//! Network entity id → ECS entity, and the server-authoritative position bookkeeping that goes
//! with it.
//!
//! java: `client/multiplayer/WorldClient.java` (`entityList`/`addEntityToWorld`/
//! `removeEntityFromWorld`), `entity/Entity.java` (`serverPosX/Y/Z`,
//! `setPositionAndRotation2`), `entity/EntityLivingBase.java` (the 3-tick interpolation).
//!
//! # Why the fixed-point position is kept
//!
//! `S15PacketEntityRelMove` and friends carry **relative** movement as three `i8`s in units of
//! 1/32 of a block. Vanilla adds those to `Entity.serverPosX/Y/Z` — integers — and only then
//! divides by 32 to get a `double`. Accumulating the deltas in floating point instead lets
//! rounding error build up over a long walk, so [`TrackedEntity::server_pos`] mirrors vanilla's
//! integers exactly and the `f64` position is always derived from them.
//! java: `NetHandlerPlayClient.handleEntityMovement` (`:613`).

use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicI64, Ordering};
use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};

